WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.018 --> 00:00:00.810
LORIN PRICE: Extra!

00:00:00.810 --> 00:00:01.570
Extra!

00:00:01.570 --> 00:00:02.910
Read all about it!

00:00:02.910 --> 00:00:07.470
A dog is now the first ever
CEO of a technology company.

00:00:07.470 --> 00:00:10.270
ANTHONY OKWECHIME: Lorin,
what are you doing?

00:00:10.270 --> 00:00:12.720
LORIN PRICE: I'm trying to
market my technology blog.

00:00:12.720 --> 00:00:15.590
But so far, I can't really
tell if it's working.

00:00:15.590 --> 00:00:17.610
ANTHONY OKWECHIME: You
know, If you transfer

00:00:17.610 --> 00:00:20.820
all of the data you collect
with Campaign Manager

00:00:20.820 --> 00:00:24.840
into Google BigQuery, you can
more easily tell what's working

00:00:24.840 --> 00:00:27.577
and what isn't when it
comes to your marketing.

00:00:27.577 --> 00:00:28.910
LORIN PRICE: Let's check it out.

00:00:28.910 --> 00:00:29.827
ANTHONY OKWECHIME: OK.

00:00:29.827 --> 00:00:31.635
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:36.010 --> 00:00:40.180
Campaign Manager is a
web-based ad management system

00:00:40.180 --> 00:00:43.090
for advertisers
and agencies that

00:00:43.090 --> 00:00:47.380
enables the management of
campaigns across the web.

00:00:47.380 --> 00:00:50.270
In order to access the
data in your account,

00:00:50.270 --> 00:00:53.170
you will need to contact
Campaign Manager support

00:00:53.170 --> 00:00:56.830
to set up the data transfer
files on your behalf.

00:00:56.830 --> 00:01:00.880
These files will be hosted in
a Google Cloud storage bucket.

00:01:00.880 --> 00:01:02.680
Let's get right to it.

00:01:02.680 --> 00:01:06.640
In the BigQuery UI, navigate
to the Transfers tab.

00:01:06.640 --> 00:01:10.870
And you can see the list of
already configured transfers.

00:01:10.870 --> 00:01:14.650
Now we're going to
create a new transfer.

00:01:14.650 --> 00:01:17.890
You can specify the
source, which in this case

00:01:17.890 --> 00:01:19.580
is Campaign Manager.

00:01:19.580 --> 00:01:21.700
You also have multiple
other Google data

00:01:21.700 --> 00:01:24.610
sources available,
from Google Ads

00:01:24.610 --> 00:01:27.460
to Cloud Storage to YouTube.

00:01:27.460 --> 00:01:31.330
We'll enter a display name,
configure any scheduling

00:01:31.330 --> 00:01:35.980
options for the transfer, and
select a destination data set.

00:01:35.980 --> 00:01:39.190
Now, in the Data
Source Details section,

00:01:39.190 --> 00:01:42.100
information about the
Cloud Storage bucket

00:01:42.100 --> 00:01:45.970
and the DoubleClick ID will be
provided by Campaign Manager

00:01:45.970 --> 00:01:47.260
support.

00:01:47.260 --> 00:01:49.870
You can also enable
notifications

00:01:49.870 --> 00:01:54.850
so that a designated individual
can be notified on transfer run

00:01:54.850 --> 00:01:56.530
failures.

00:01:56.530 --> 00:01:59.740
Once you click Save, the
data transfer process

00:01:59.740 --> 00:02:03.730
will begin on a preset schedule.

00:02:03.730 --> 00:02:06.790
Let's switch to
Existing Transfers.

00:02:06.790 --> 00:02:09.550
Select our recently
created transfer,

00:02:09.550 --> 00:02:12.970
and then click on Configuration.

00:02:12.970 --> 00:02:16.360
You can see that the
source is Campaign Manager,

00:02:16.360 --> 00:02:20.380
and there isn't a schedule
as we didn't create one.

00:02:20.380 --> 00:02:26.180
Also, the destination data
set we selected is displayed.

00:02:26.180 --> 00:02:29.680
You can click on Run
History to view information

00:02:29.680 --> 00:02:32.080
about the daily runs of
the transfer schedule.

00:02:35.110 --> 00:02:39.220
Next, we'll examine the data
sets where our Campaign Manager

00:02:39.220 --> 00:02:44.380
data lives and take a look at
one of the impressions tables.

00:02:44.380 --> 00:02:47.920
We can see a sample of
the impressions table data

00:02:47.920 --> 00:02:49.930
by clicking Preview.

00:02:49.930 --> 00:02:54.820
This table has columns for
advertiser ID, campaign ID,

00:02:54.820 --> 00:02:59.720
country code, states or
region, and much more.

00:02:59.720 --> 00:03:03.130
Now, let's take a look at one
of the match tables, which

00:03:03.130 --> 00:03:06.520
is essentially a lookup
table for the IDs you

00:03:06.520 --> 00:03:10.090
saw in the impressions table.

00:03:10.090 --> 00:03:12.460
You can see that
in the match table,

00:03:12.460 --> 00:03:16.240
the campaign IDs are matched
to the corresponding campaign

00:03:16.240 --> 00:03:16.890
name.

00:03:16.890 --> 00:03:20.140
LORIN PRICE: Well, all of
my data is now in one place,

00:03:20.140 --> 00:03:21.910
but it still doesn't
tell me anything new.

00:03:21.910 --> 00:03:23.033
Now what?

00:03:23.033 --> 00:03:23.950
ANTHONY OKWECHIME: OK.

00:03:23.950 --> 00:03:27.140
Now that all of your
data is in BigQuery,

00:03:27.140 --> 00:03:29.110
we have the ability
to run queries on it

00:03:29.110 --> 00:03:33.010
to make connections that
aren't visible by just looking

00:03:33.010 --> 00:03:34.168
at the data.

00:03:34.168 --> 00:03:35.710
LORIN PRICE: What
are we waiting for?

00:03:35.710 --> 00:03:39.050
Stories like dog CEOs
need to be heard.

00:03:39.050 --> 00:03:42.340
ANTHONY OKWECHIME: Let's begin
to see what kinds of insights

00:03:42.340 --> 00:03:46.910
a digital marketer might
want from this data.

00:03:46.910 --> 00:03:49.840
A good example would
be to run a query

00:03:49.840 --> 00:03:53.890
to provide the number of
impressions and unique users

00:03:53.890 --> 00:03:58.560
by campaign from the
beginning of this year.

00:03:58.560 --> 00:03:59.920
Let's do that.

00:03:59.920 --> 00:04:02.710
The results show us just that.

00:04:02.710 --> 00:04:06.480
Notice that we only see
Campaign Manager IDs, though,

00:04:06.480 --> 00:04:08.890
and no campaign names.

00:04:08.890 --> 00:04:11.890
Now, let's take this
one step further

00:04:11.890 --> 00:04:16.959
by executing a join on the
impressions and match tables

00:04:16.959 --> 00:04:19.810
and running the same query.

00:04:19.810 --> 00:04:23.350
This time, we can see the
Campaign Manager names

00:04:23.350 --> 00:04:26.890
by date in addition to
the impression count

00:04:26.890 --> 00:04:29.890
and the number of unique users.

00:04:29.890 --> 00:04:33.340
Marketing teams find
this type of information

00:04:33.340 --> 00:04:35.050
extremely useful.

00:04:35.050 --> 00:04:37.690
And all of these
insights and more

00:04:37.690 --> 00:04:41.080
can be found by using the
Campaign Manager data transfer

00:04:41.080 --> 00:04:43.600
service with BigQuery.

00:04:43.600 --> 00:04:45.070
Looks like you're doing well.

00:04:45.070 --> 00:04:47.403
LORIN PRICE: Now that I know
I can use the BigQuery data

00:04:47.403 --> 00:04:49.540
transfer service with
Campaign Manager data,

00:04:49.540 --> 00:04:52.060
I am able to customize my
marketing campaigns much

00:04:52.060 --> 00:04:54.400
better, and I have
more readers than ever.

00:04:54.400 --> 00:04:56.650
ANTHONY OKWECHIME: And
how's that dog CEO doing?

00:04:56.650 --> 00:04:59.460
LORIN PRICE: You'll have to stop
by the blog to check it out.

00:04:59.460 --> 00:05:01.210
ANTHONY OKWECHIME:
Thank you for watching.

00:05:01.210 --> 00:05:03.250
LORIN PRICE: Don't forget to
subscribe for more great Google

00:05:03.250 --> 00:05:04.510
Cloud Platform content.

00:05:04.510 --> 00:05:06.960
[MUSIC PLAYING]

